> I go up to Nefret and am told to keep the Eye!  Now my question is I
> can't tell from the spoiler (on steelypips which seems to be working
> briefly but again seems to be down)  if I wear the thing does it still
> confer magic resistance and faster energy regeneration as well as half
> damage when attacked by spells, or is that only if I carry it?

The benefit you get from wearing the Eye (as opposed to carrying it)
is telepathy (use if you haven't eaten a floating eye yet).

I would try the invoking power once (teleport to sokoban), just so you
get to
see what it does.  Remember to have your pets next to you just like
when
moving up/down stairs.


> Am I ready for Medusa?

Yes.  As long as you are blind(folded), Medusa can't really harm you
(don't try eating her corse though).  If you don't have a form of
levitation,
I would recommend digging down and coming up the stairs.  (You can
always use the Eye to teleport back).  Medusa's level is known for many

YASDs, and I would hazard a guess that most of those are _not_ due
to the namesake of the level.  Hint:  Kill things from far away, and
have a
wand of teleport handy to teleport away monsters that get too.....
...affectionate.   Remember that you can always use the Eye to get
away.

> After Medusa I have to find Vlad's tower right and get the candle
> holder for the 7 candles I already have.  Yes? What exactly should I
> expect.  Please spoil me completely.

The next big step after Medusa are two levels one after the other known
as "The Castle" and "The Valley of the Dead".  Both are full of
monsters
and also have numerous opportunities for useful items.  In particular,
the Castle always has a wand of wishing somewhere.    My advice is
not to wish for anything until you've cleared out both those levels
(and read the wishing spoilers).  You'll get upset after wishing for
say a Helm of Brilliance only to find one in one of the storerooms.

I would suggest that at least for the first time too each of these
levels you
come fully prepared to face anything (i.e.  have a set of defensive and
offensive wands in your main inventory - not in a bag of holding for
example),
have your favorite spells ready (esp. Charm Monster - it practically
makes
you need to kill only half of the monsters on the level).  I would try
and charm
a few good pets in the levels above the castle.   They don't help that
much,
but they take up slots should you get mobbed.

Use Magicbane.  Unless you have enough scrolls of enchant weapon to get
something like Fire Brand up to something like +5, I would stick to
that Athame.  It will absorb most curses sent your way, and as a wizard
with
the Eye you really can rely on your spells (and your pets).

Elbereth.  But you know that arleady.

Basically, take it slowly.  The Castle has a moat, doors, traps, etc.
You
can clear one set of enemies at a time if you do things carefully.  You
don't
have that many hitpoints, and your AC could use an improvment (try
putting
on a small shield - see how much it reduces your spellcasting.  It
might
not hurt you that much if you're only casting FB and CM). Getting
across
the moat and into the Castle proper is not simple, though there
are quite a few ways of doing it.

One last warning.
Some people regard the drawbridge of the Castle as a weapon of mass
destruction.  

Good Luck.

